{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543748,"nonce":"piAtsKj1aXtsrZtA5dRIaM+yo0g=","target":"6110c2898e342cdb8f8c3882ae7dca6b53f287b72194b16748c2d3a4d15f7daa","message":"After one coupled solve in which a `jno.fem` participant receives a boundary term (`K1*fa + 5*(ua - ub)` on its right edge), a second `jno._coupling` `couple([...])` built from the same expressions finds the fem side bound to the tag `gauss_right` instead of `right`. It fails with `interface: the domain has no boundary facets tagged gauss_right`.\n\n**Reproduce.** Use the problems `_fem_cold` and `_fvm_hot` of `tests/test_coupling_transient.py`: couple them once with a boundary term on the fem side, then build a second coupling from the same problem objects and expressions.\n\n**Cause.** The fem build of a boundary integral mutates the Variable objects of the user expression in place. `_retag_coords_for_quadrature` in `jno/_fem.py` sets `v.tag = \"gauss_\u003cregion\u003e\"` and stores the original in `v._jno_region_tag`. `jno/_fem.py` (`_region_and_support`) and some other readers undo this through `_jno_region_tag`. The coupling driver does not: `jno/_coupling/driver.py` (`_analyse`) collects `v.tag` of the bound Variables directly, so it sees `gauss_right`.\n\n**Workaround.** Build fresh problems per coupling.\n\n**Direction.** The smallest fix is for the driver to read the region the way `_effective_tag` does (`_jno_region_tag` when the tag is `fem_gauss` or `gauss_*`), including in `_tags_from_variables`. The cleaner one stops the in-place mutation of user Variables during the fem build, but the retag must persist for lazy nonlinear and transient operators that re-read `.tag` at call time, so that change is larger. A regression test would couple twice from the same expressions.\n\nCited as git-bug 44958ea in the commit history.","files":null}]}